body:

**Last updated:** Aug 2026

## Introduction

The Mountaineering Acoustics Network (MAN) is committed to protecting your privacy and ensuring transparency in how we handle your data. This privacy policy explains our practices regarding data collection, usage, and protection.

## Our Services

Our primary business activities are:
- Publishing team news and research on our website
- Providing academic and research information
- Maintaining a professional network platform

## Data Collection

**We do not collect personal data from visitors to our website.**

### No Cookies
Our website does not use cookies or any form of tracking technology.

### No Analytics
We do not use third-party analytics services or tracking tools.

### No Registration Required
We do not offer public registration for any services on our servers. All information is provided publicly without requiring user accounts.

## Technical Implementation

### Self-Hosted JavaScript
All JavaScript code on our website is self-hosted. We do not load external scripts from third-party services.

### Server Information
For debugging purposes only, we maintain standard nginx server logs. These logs contain:
- IP addresses
- Access timestamps
- Requested URLs
- HTTP status codes

These logs are used solely for technical maintenance and are not shared with third parties.

## Data Storage

**We do not store any personal information on our servers.**

- No user accounts or profiles
- No contact form submissions
- No newsletter subscriptions
- No personal data collection

## Third-Party Services

We do not integrate with any third-party services that would collect user data.

## Contact Information

For privacy-related inquiries, please contact us at:
- Email: [gewenxin.yu@proton.me](mailto:gewenxin.yu@proton.me)
- Matrix: [@g.yu:matrix.org](https://matrix.to/#/@g.yu:matrix.org)

## Updates

This privacy policy may be updated periodically. Any significant changes will be announced on our website.

---

*This privacy policy reflects our commitment to transparency and minimal data collection.*
